  "Born Eugene Gladstone O'Neill in a Broadway hotel in New York on 16 October 1888, the son of the actor James O'Neill; he went to sea, worked as a reporter, and made his name with Beyond the Horizon (1920, Pulitzer Prize). The Emperor Jones (1920), Anna Christie (1921), Desire Under the Elms (1924), Strange Interlude (1928), Mourning Becomes Electra (1931) and Long Day's Journey into Night (written 1940) followed. He died in Boston on 27 November 1953.",
