  "Novelist, poet and journalist; the author of Nightwood, one of the great modernist novels in English.",
  "Born Djuna Chappell Barnes at Cornwall-on-Hudson, New York, on 12 June 1892; she worked as a journalist in New York, published A Book (1923), Ryder (1928) and Ladies Almanack (1928), and moved to Paris in 1921, where Nightwood was written and published in 1936 with an introduction by T. S. Eliot. She returned to New York and lived as a recluse until her death on 18 June 1982.",
  "Djuna Barnes wrote Nightwood, one of the great modernist novels in English and the finest novel of the expatriate lesbian Paris: its prose, its portrait of Robin Vote and its exploration of sexual obsession made it a central text of modernism, admired by Eliot, Joyce and the whole expatriate avant-garde.",
